<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Omri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned c. 884–873 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Omri was king of Israel from about 884 to 873 BCE and founder of a dynasty that included his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>. A general who won the throne in a civil war, he built a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> and made Israel a regional power, subduing Moab, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele">Mesha Stele</a> records. Assyrian texts long afterwards called Israel 'the house of Omri'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omri.jpg" alt="Omri" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume Rouille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omri_King.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
